{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310721,"nonce":"omds5SsyviVbc6ujBwOVR2Eg4XY=","title":"SwiGLU gate+up matmul fusion (mirror of fusedQkvMatmul) implemented end-to-end 2026-06-06 — runtime fn + plugin registration + MIR wrapper ...","message":"Voronoi 900-tok profile (CPU sampling via SIGPROF, `tools/profile_run.sh`)\nshowed **70.4% of Haxe-attributed samples in `nue.Linear.forward`\n(Linear.hx:65)** — the matmul dispatch. Per token: 7 Linear calls per\nlayer × 16 layers = 112 matmul invocations. The QKV path already\nshares one X→Q8_K pre-quant + one `parallel_rows` fork-join across\nthree weights via `fusedQkvMatmul` (commit `139c4f5` family). SwiGLU's\ngate+up have the same structural shape (same activation, two\nindependent weight matrices) but ran as two separate\n`matmulXTQThreaded` calls — redundant pre-quant + redundant\nfork-join. The hypothesis was that fusing gate+up the same way QKV\nis fused would save 1 pre-quant + 1 fork-join per layer per token,\nestimated 3-5% decode wall.\n\n\nFull plumbing landed end-to-end (≈200 LOC):\n\n- `runtime/src/quant.rs`:\n  `rayzor_tensor_matmul_gate_up_qt_t_f32_threaded` — mirror of\n  `rayzor_tensor_matmul_qkv_qt_t_f32_threaded` with 2 outputs.\n  Concat-row layout `[0, g_n + u_n)`; one shared `prepare_x_q8k_blocks`\n  call; one `worker_pool::global().parallel_rows(...)` fan-out.\n- `runtime/src/plugin_impl.rs`: `register_symbol!` entry.\n- `compiler/src/stdlib/qtensor.rs`: extern declaration in\n  `declare_externs` + `build_qtensor_fused_gate_up_matmul` MIR\n  wrapper that allocates a 2-element `Array\u003cTensor\u003e` and pushes\n  gate/up handles. Wired into `build_qtensor` builder list.\n- `compiler/src/stdlib/runtime_mapping.rs`: `map_method!(static\n  \"rayzor_ds_QTensor\", \"fusedGateUpMatmul\" =\u003e ...)`.\n- `compiler/haxe-std/rayzor/ds/QTensor.hx`: extern method\n  declaration with `@:native(\"tensor_matmul_gate_up_qt_t_f32_threaded\")`.\n- `nue/nue/transformer/SwiGLU.hx`: use the fused path when both\n  gate and up are Q4_K_M; fall back to two sequential `Linear.forward`\n  calls otherwise.\n\nBuild clean, Paris MATCH preserved byte-exact.\n\n\nVoronoi 900-tok prompt, N=300, alternating (with stash dance on\nSwiGLU.hx because the binary compiles Haxe at runtime):\n\n```\nCOOL pair:    A (fused) = 31.57 tok/s   B (pre-fused) = 31.64 tok/s   Δ -0.2%\nTHROTTLED pair: A (fused) = 12.93 tok/s   B (pre-fused) = 12.78 tok/s   Δ +1.2%\n```\n\nAt cool steady state the fusion delivers **essentially zero\nimprovement** (sub-0.5%, indistinguishable from noise). At\nthermally-throttled state there's maybe a 1% advantage but it's\nwithin the per-run variance.\n\n\nI estimated each pre-quant at ~100μs scalar work and the parallel_rows\nsync at ~50μs. The actual numbers are smaller:\n\n- `prepare_x_q8k_blocks` for k=2048 scans 2048 floats + writes 8\n  Q8_K blocks. NEON-friendly inner loop in the runtime. Probably\n  ~10-30μs, not 100μs.\n- `worker_pool::global().parallel_rows` uses a persistent pool, so\n  no thread spawn cost per call — only wake + condvar + join. ~10-30μs.\n\nTotal cost per matmul call: ~20-60μs of overhead vs ~200-300μs of\nactual compute. Halving the overhead for 1 of 7 calls per layer\nsaves at most 16 × 30μs = 480μs per token. At 33ms/token (30 tok/s)\nthat's 1.4% — at the floor of detectability and consistent with\nwhat the bench actually measured.\n\n\nThis is the **third optimization** in this session that mirrored a\nproven-winning pattern from a different layer and washed out:\n\n1. [[bugs-q6k-partial-unroll-no-win]] — Q6_K partial-acc unroll\n   mirroring Q4_K_M's a241cc9 — no win because Q6_K's dense unpack\n   already hides the serial dependency\n2. [[bugs-f32-flash-gqa-no-win]] — F32 flash GQA-aware restructure\n   mirroring Q8 a490a3b — no win because L1 already absorbs the\n   redundant K/V loads\n3. **This** — SwiGLU gate+up fusion mirroring QKV fusion — no win\n   because the pre-quant + sync costs were smaller than estimated\n\nThe common thread: **the previous winning optimization succeeded\nbecause of a SPECIFIC hardware/runtime characteristic that doesn't\ngeneralize to the analogous case.** I keep estimating overhead by\nintuition and overshooting. The real cost is usually 2-5× smaller\nthan my intuition says.\n\nFor future perf work in this codebase: **measure the specific\noverhead with a microbench BEFORE writing a 100+ LOC fix.** The\npattern of \"mirror an existing pattern\" sounds safe but the win\nisn't guaranteed by the architectural symmetry — it depends on\nthe actual per-overhead cost being a real fraction of total time.\n\n\n- The QKV fusion (`fusedQkvMatmul` at commit `139c4f5` family) is\n  KEPT because Q+K+V have three weights with very different N\n  values (q_n=2048, k_n=512, v_n=512 for Llama-3.2-1B). The\n  concat-row layout with parallel_rows over [0, 3072) is a real\n  load-balancing win for the threaded path — 6 workers each get\n  ~512 rows. Without the concat, the K and V matmuls would each\n  spawn 6 workers for 512 rows (85 rows each), which is worse\n  scheduling. Gate+up have IDENTICAL N (intermediate_size, both\n  ~5504 for Llama-3.2-1B) so the per-matmul row count is already\n  big enough that 6 workers get ~917 rows each without help.\n- [[perf-q4km-sdot-4way-unroll]] — DID win (+6.95% long-form)\n- [[bugs-q4km-sdot-tile-no-win]] — same washout pattern\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_swiglu_fusion_no_win` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}